WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.658
What if you could teach a spacecraft to land on the Moon with just thirty-six numbers?

00:00:05.658 --> 00:00:08.142
No neural network, and no gradients.

00:00:08.142 --> 00:00:09.741
Only evolution.

00:00:10.341 --> 00:00:12.117
This is Lunar Lander.

00:00:12.117 --> 00:00:15.977
The goal is to touch down softly between the two flags.

00:00:15.977 --> 00:00:21.116
Gravity pulls the lander down, and every burst of an engine costs fuel.

00:00:21.716 --> 00:00:25.122
At every step, the lander senses eight numbers.

00:00:25.122 --> 00:00:32.543
Where it is, how fast it is moving, how it is tilted and spinning, and whether each leg touches the ground.

00:00:33.143 --> 00:00:36.823
And at every step, it chooses one of four actions.

00:00:36.823 --> 00:00:42.769
Do nothing, fire the left engine, fire the main engine, or fire the right engine.

00:00:43.369 --> 00:00:46.343
The pilot is one small table of numbers.

00:00:46.343 --> 00:00:52.290
Each of the eight inputs is multiplied by a weight for each action, and a bias is added.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.510
That gives four scores.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:57.993
The action with the highest score fires.

00:00:57.993 --> 00:01:01.836
Watch the scores rise and fall as the lander flies.

00:01:02.436 --> 00:01:07.250
Eight times four weights, plus four biases, makes thirty-six numbers.

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:12.114
Finding good values for them is the job of the genetic algorithm.

00:01:12.714 --> 00:01:16.024
We start with a population of random pilots.

00:01:16.024 --> 00:01:19.571
Each one is just thirty-six random numbers.

00:01:20.171 --> 00:01:23.210
Every pilot flies, and gets a fitness score.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:24.847
Random pilots crash.

00:01:24.847 --> 00:01:28.595
Some of them crash a little less badly than others.

00:01:29.195 --> 00:01:30.943
The best half are kept.

00:01:30.943 --> 00:01:34.458
They decide where the next generation comes from.

00:01:35.058 --> 00:01:36.773
That's the whole loop.

00:01:36.773 --> 00:01:41.634
Sample a population, fly every pilot, rank them, and move toward the winners.

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:42.777
Then repeat.

00:01:42.777 --> 00:01:44.721
Nothing is differentiated.

00:01:44.721 --> 00:01:47.766
The algorithm only ever sees the scores.

00:01:48.366 --> 00:01:51.161
The algorithm we use is called CMA-ES.

00:01:51.161 --> 00:01:56.203
Here it is on a toy problem with only two numbers, so we can watch it work.

00:01:56.203 --> 00:01:59.298
Dark means good, and bright means bad.

00:01:59.898 --> 00:02:04.239
It keeps a cloud of candidates, shaped like a stretched bell curve.

00:02:04.239 --> 00:02:10.386
Each generation it samples new points, keeps the best half, and moves the centre toward them.

00:02:10.986 --> 00:02:13.851
It also learns the shape of the problem.

00:02:13.851 --> 00:02:20.298
The cloud stretches along the valley, races down it, and shrinks as it closes in on the answer.

00:02:20.898 --> 00:02:22.780
How do we score a pilot?

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:27.485
It flies ten episodes, and we start from its average reward in the game.

00:02:27.485 --> 00:02:31.020
That already punishes crashing and wasted fuel.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:37.092
Then we add a big bonus for landing, and a small one for landing quickly.

00:02:37.692 --> 00:02:39.825
Now let's watch it learn.

00:02:39.825 --> 00:02:45.359
Each dot is one pilot, and each column is one generation of fifty pilots.

00:02:45.959 --> 00:02:50.403
In the first thirty generations, nearly every pilot still crashes.

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:57.309
But the dots keep creeping upward, because each generation is sampled around the best of the last one.

00:02:57.909 --> 00:03:00.434
Then the whole population lifts.

00:03:00.434 --> 00:03:03.780
By generation seventy-five, most pilots land.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:13.360
The orange line is the best pilot, the blue line is the average, and the gap between them closes as the population settles on one strategy.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.535
Here is the same story in flight.

00:03:16.535 --> 00:03:23.742
All fifty pilots of one generation start from exactly the same spot, and we draw the path of each one.

00:03:24.342 --> 00:03:26.410
Generation zero is chaos.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:29.981
Pilots fall straight down, or fly off the screen.

00:03:29.981 --> 00:03:33.915
Five generations later, not one of them lands yet.

00:03:34.515 --> 00:03:38.252
By generation ten, a few paths bend toward the pad.

00:03:38.252 --> 00:03:40.659
By forty, more of them arrive.

00:03:40.659 --> 00:03:44.506
And by fifty, nineteen of the fifty pilots land.

00:03:45.515 --> 00:03:49.266
By generation one hundred, forty-eight of them land.

00:03:49.266 --> 00:03:55.506
The whole population flies almost the same path, a smooth curve down between the flags.

00:03:56.106 --> 00:03:58.748
This is the population as points.

00:03:58.748 --> 00:04:05.105
Each pilot is thirty-six numbers, flattened down to two, so each dot is a whole pilot.

00:04:05.705 --> 00:04:12.007
The centre of the cloud travels a long way from where it started, toward the region where pilots land.

00:04:12.007 --> 00:04:14.312
The white line is its path.

00:04:14.912 --> 00:04:17.074
And there's a neat trick here.

00:04:17.074 --> 00:04:22.651
Only the highest score matters, so making every weight bigger doesn't change any decision.

00:04:22.651 --> 00:04:29.893
As the weights grow, the same step size becomes a smaller and smaller step, and the search turns into fine-tuning.

00:04:30.493 --> 00:04:34.971
Here is the centre of the cloud, drawn as the weight matrix itself.

00:04:34.971 --> 00:04:37.300
At generation zero it is noise.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.570
Watch the pattern appear.

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:42.728
The strongest rules are about speed.

00:04:42.728 --> 00:04:45.577
Drifting sideways, falling, and spinning.

00:04:45.577 --> 00:04:49.482
They are exactly the things a human pilot would watch.

00:04:50.082 --> 00:04:55.998
Here are the best pilots from different points in training, all facing the same start.

00:04:56.598 --> 00:05:00.946
The random pilot flies away, and the early champion crashes.

00:05:00.946 --> 00:05:06.616
By generation thirty the best pilot lands, and later ones land more cleanly.

00:05:09.223 --> 00:05:13.311
Because the pilot is so simple, we can follow every decision.

00:05:13.311 --> 00:05:15.681
Here the lander is falling fast.

00:05:15.681 --> 00:05:18.940
Each input times its weight adds to each score.

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:24.987
The falling speed pushes the main engine up and do nothing down, so the main engine wins.

00:05:25.587 --> 00:05:28.772
A moment earlier, it was tilted to the right.

00:05:28.772 --> 00:05:35.082
Now the left engine collects just enough votes to win, and it turns the lander back upright.

00:05:35.682 --> 00:05:38.005
We can draw a whole rule at once.

00:05:38.005 --> 00:05:41.178
Across is the tilt, and up and down is the spin.

00:05:41.178 --> 00:05:44.707
Each colour is the action the pilot would choose.

00:05:45.307 --> 00:05:47.666
The white line is a real flight.

00:05:47.666 --> 00:05:53.391
The pilot stays near the middle, hopping between the engines to keep itself upright.

00:05:53.991 --> 00:05:56.096
So what did evolution find?

00:05:56.096 --> 00:05:59.523
The main engine fires when the lander is falling.

00:05:59.523 --> 00:06:04.453
Its bias is negative, so it stays off unless the fall is fast enough.

00:06:05.053 --> 00:06:12.130
Do nothing gets a strong vote when the lander is rising, and a strong vote against it when the left leg is down.

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:14.966
This pilot almost never just waits.

00:06:15.566 --> 00:06:18.434
The left engine fixes drift and spin.

00:06:18.434 --> 00:06:24.852
It fires when the lander drifts right or spins clockwise, and it pushes the lander back.

00:06:25.452 --> 00:06:28.127
And the right engine is the default.

00:06:28.127 --> 00:06:33.722
It fires almost half the time, with small, cheap pulses whenever no other rule wins.

00:06:33.722 --> 00:06:40.224
When the lander drifts left, the left engine loses its votes, and the right engine takes over.

00:06:40.824 --> 00:06:48.020
Tested on one hundred new landings it never saw in training, this pilot lands ninety-six times.

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:52.946
All of it from thirty-six numbers and a loop of trial and error.

00:06:52.946 --> 00:06:54.748
The code is on GitHub.

00:06:54.748 --> 00:06:57.932
Run it, and watch your own pilot evolve.
